  "grammar_explanations": [
    {
      "slug": "past simple: regular verbs -ed (review)",
      "title_form": "Pasado simple: verbos regulares con -ed (repaso)",
      "title_target": "Past simple: regular verbs with -ed (review)",
      "description_md_form": "El **pasado simple** se usa para hablar de acciones que ya terminaron. Para verbos regulares, solo agregamos **-ed** al final del verbo.\n\n**Reglas de ortografía:**\n- Verbo normal: `offer` → `offered`\n- Verbo que termina en **-e**: `like` → `liked`\n- Verbo que termina en **consonante + y**: `study` → `studied`\n- Verbo corto con **consonante + vocal + consonante**: `stop` → `stopped`\n\n**Forma negativa:** usamos `did not` (o `didn't`) + verbo en forma base.\n- `I didn't like the shirt.`\n\n**Forma interrogativa:** usamos `Did` + sujeto + verbo en forma base.\n- `Did you buy it?`\n\n**Palabras clave:** `yesterday`, `last week`, `two days ago`.\n\n**Ejemplo:** `The store offered me a refund.` (La tienda me ofreció un reembolso.)",
      "description_md_target": "The **past simple** is used to talk about actions that are already finished. For regular verbs, we just add **-ed** to the end of the verb.\n\n**Spelling rules:**\n- Normal verb: `offer` → `offered`\n- Verb ending in **-e**: `like` → `liked`\n- Verb ending in **consonant + y**: `study` → `studied`\n- Short verb with **consonant + vowel + consonant**: `stop` → `stopped`\n\n**Negative form:** we use `did not` (or `didn't`) + base form of the verb.\n- `I didn't like the shirt.`\n\n**Question form:** we use `Did` + subject + base form of the verb.\n- `Did you buy it?`\n\n**Key words:** `yesterday`, `last week`, `two days ago`.\n\n**Example:** `The store offered me a refund.`",
      "grammar_structure": "Subject + verb + -ed (affirmative) / Subject + did not + base verb (negative) / Did + subject + base verb? (question)",
      "conjugation_table": {
        "name": "past_simple_regular",
